### Why this should work

Your dependency chain is:

```text
user-service
     │
     ├── express
     │
     └── @packages/logger
              │
              └── packages/logger
```

Therefore Docker needs:

```text
package.json
pnpm-workspace.yaml
pnpm-lock.yaml
       │
       ├── apps/user-service/package.json
       │
       └── packages/logger/
```

The Dockerfile now copies exactly those pieces before running:

```bash
pnpm install --filter user-service...
```

The `...` is important here because it tells pnpm to include the workspace dependencies required by `user-service`.

### One thing I would change

You currently have:

```text
apps/user-service/node_modules/
```

and also:

```text
6_ecommerce_monorepo_nodejs/node_modules/
```

You **should not copy either `node_modules` directory into the Docker image**.

Add a root `.dockerignore`:

```text
node_modules
**/node_modules
.git
.gitignore
_Note
*.log
```

Then your Docker build remains clean:

```text
Host
 │
 ├── node_modules        ← ignored
 ├── apps/*/node_modules ← ignored
 │
 └── Docker build
       │
       ├── package.json
       ├── pnpm-lock.yaml
       ├── pnpm-workspace.yaml
       ├── apps/user-service/package.json
       ├── apps/user-service/src
       └── packages/
```

**One final recommendation:** don't copy the entire `apps/user-service` directory with
 `COPY apps/user-service ./apps/user-service`, because that would potentially copy its local
  `node_modules`. Your current `COPY apps/user-service/src ...` is cleaner.
